.\" Manual page for doorman, the Call Me Maybe lobby daemon.
.\" Render locally with:  man ./docs/doorman.1
.TH DOORMAN 1 "2026-08-10" "Call Me Maybe" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
doorman \- lobby daemon and operations tool for a Call Me Maybe home phone
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B doorman
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.PP
.B doorman
.RI "# with no subcommand, runs the service"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B doorman
decides who gets connected. Asterisk handles SIP, RTP, and DTMF detection;
.B doorman
talks to it over ARI on loopback and applies policy. One call is one
goroutine, and the caller hanging up cancels a context that unwinds
everything.
.PP
Known callers hear a welcome prompt and ring the whole house; a digit pressed
over that prompt reaches the extension collector instead, so an allow\-listed
caller can ask for one room rather than all of them. Pressing nothing rings
the house the moment the prompt ends, and nothing done at the keypad can cost
an allow\-listed caller that ring.
.PP
A caller in the account's lobby pass book (below) is a known caller too,
welcomed by the contact's name, unless
.B [[people]]
names the number first.
.PP
Everyone else meets the lobby: dial a 6\-digit extension within the dial
window, or get whatever
.B [line] on_no_input
says \(em by default the bouncer says "Good day" and hangs up.
.PP
A public line answers with a front menu instead:
.B [line] menu
names a
.B [[menus]]
entry, and each
.B [[menus.choices]]
key leads to an extension (by its
.BR id ,
when it may have no PIN and is never in the PIN lookup), a recording, a
second menu, a mailbox, or a shelf of the audiobook library. The PIN lobby
never runs on such a line. A choice
with
.BR "challenge = true" ,
and
.B [line] challenge_no_input
in front of
.BR on_no_input ,
ask a spoken sum before anything rings or records: a filter for
robodialers and very young children, not age verification, and closed to a
caller who cannot press keys. Wrong answers spend the line's own challenge
budget, never the PIN one, and a right answer forgives nothing. Menus nest
two deep; a call gets six plays or three minutes, then what silence gets.
Callers the ladder admits skip both.
.PP
Dial\-a\-Story:
.B [[shelves]]
lists the catalog ids of the audiobooks a line offers \(em never the whole
library \(em and a choice with
.B shelf
browses one while
.B story_of_the_day
plays one title from it, a different one each day, and hangs up. doorman
releases the caller into the generated
.B [cmm\-listen]
context, which runs the same books menu
.B *2665
does with the shelf's titles alone, so a title off the shelf cannot be
reached.
.B [listening] max_minutes
(default 20, from when the call rang) ends the call, with a spoken warning
one minute before;
.B max_listeners
(default 1) caps listeners on the line, and a caller over it hears that the
listening lines are busy and the menu again. Outside callers get no
bookmarks.
.PP
Outbound, a handset presents the caller ID of the line it belongs to:
.B [line] outbound_cid
on the policy file whose
.B [line] outbound_handsets
claims it, or on plain
.I policy.toml
\(em the primary line \(em for a handset no line claims. Dialling
.B *4
answers with a menu of the numbers this box can call as and places one call as
whichever is chosen. With a
.I trunks.toml
the call also leaves by that line's
.BR "[line] trunk" ,
because a provider will not present a number its account does not own; the
caller ID and the trunk are one decision and travel together on both paths.
.B *78NN
quiets the phone that dialled it for 15, 30 or 45 minutes and
.B *79
ends it: room calls say how long and offer its box, the ring\-all and the
page skip it, except a page from a handset with
.B page_override
in
.IR handsets.toml .
The state is Asterisk's own, time\-boxed, read by doorman and never written.
.B curfew
on a handset in
.I handsets.toml
names
.B [[schedules]]
ids from
.IR policy.toml ;
while any is active the phone is asleep: doorman rings it for nothing, the
generated dialplan leaves it out of the ring\-all and the page (a
.B page_override
does not pierce it) and refuses its calls out, 911 and the reminder apps
excepted, and the daemon drops ordinary calls at the hour. Several ids make
a per\-day bedtime. A dangling id fails
.BR "doorman check" ;
the hours ride in the dialplan, so a change needs
.B doorman render
and a reload.
.B *80
schedules a call today at an AM/PM time;
.B *81
schedules a call in 0\(en9 hours and 0\(en99 minutes (minutes carry into hours);
.B *82
schedules a one\-shot wake\-up call tomorrow. Times use the appliance timezone.
Each menu lists this handset's pending calls and offers cancellation, then
accepts a time and an optional recorded message. Callbacks ring this handset
for 30 seconds, with two retries three minutes apart. Asterisk's durable call
files survive restarts. They bypass server quiet/curfew rules but respect
handset DND and never auto\-answer. Standard prompts are bundled and free.
.B doorman reminders prune
removes finished recordings as the Asterisk user, hourly through
.BR doorman-reminders.timer ;
.B --spool
selects a non\-default Asterisk spool directory (default /var/spool/asterisk).
The menu/deliver AGI modes are invoked by the generated dialplan, not operators.
.B *2665
opens Books: nine recordings or chapters per page, pound for the next page
(or the page picker on the last page), star to repeat, zero to go back.
.B doorman books prepare --source catalog.json
prepares existing local recordings and offline spoken menu labels.
It requires FFmpeg and espeak-ng;
.B --library
defaults to /var/spool/asterisk/cmm-books/library.
.B doorman books prompts --library directory [--voice Samantha]
renders complete menu pages offline with macOS say and FFmpeg;
.B --shelf id,id
(once per shelf) renders a public line's shelf pages too.
A staging copy of the prepared catalog.json is sufficient. Install menu-audio
first and menus.json last; rebuild after catalog changes.
.B books prepare
moves a stale menus.json aside to menus.json.stale, and calls fall back to
the compatibility prompts until it is rebuilt. The voice must appear in
say \-v '?'. No runtime synthesis
or speech API is used. See docs/BOOKS.md.
.B doorman books list
prints that prepared catalog as JSON.
The AGI menu uses ASTSPOOLDIR and needs a private, Asterisk-writable
cmm-books/progress directory beside the library.
During playback, 1/3 changes chapter, 2/8 changes speed by 0.1x,
4/6 seeks ten seconds, 5 pauses, star repeats help and zero returns
to the book menu. Speed ranges from 0.5x to 2.0x without changing pitch.
Progress belongs to the handset, language and recording revision.
Books respects curfew and needs no cloud connection or persistent service.
.B doorman books listen
is the AGI
.B [cmm\-listen]
runs for an outside listener; it reads only the channel variables doorman
set and keeps no bookmark.
.B *88
adds a number to the phone that dialled it: the digits, then the spoken
name; the number is in that phone's directory within a minute and named
once
.B doorman phonebook
has had the recording transcribed at
.BR STT_ENDPOINT .
A directory, never the allow\-list.
.B [line] failover
is the ordered list of other trunks a call as that line tries when its own
answers CHANUNAVAIL or CONGESTION \(em off unless written, because each rung
presents that trunk's own number rather than the line's, and the call ends
in that trunk's generated context so the journal records which one carried
it. Busy, no answer and a hangup are answers, not failures, and never climb
it.
.PP
That console refuses emergency numbers outright: E911 is registered per DID
against a street address, so
.B 911
is dialled directly from a handset, leaves by the designated
.B emergency_trunk
(or the primary line's, unset), falls over to any other trunk if that one
cannot carry it, and never presents a line's caller ID.
.PP
It is also its own operations tool. Every subcommand below runs against the
same configuration and the same validator the daemon uses, so what
.B doorman check
accepts is exactly what the daemon will accept.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B doorman
Run the service. Configuration comes from the environment; see
.B ENVIRONMENT
below and
.IR examples/.env.example .
Exits non\-zero if ARI is unreachable at startup, which is deliberate: a
lobby daemon that cannot answer calls should fail loudly.
.TP
.BI "doorman check [\-handsets " path "] [\-trunks " path "] [\-contacts " path "] [\-fetch] [" path ]
Validate
.IR policy.toml ,
.I handsets.toml
and, when there are any,
.I trunks.toml
and
.I contacts.toml
together and report what they add up to \(em allow\-list size, extension
count, PIN length, the house ring group, and then every extension with each
of its settings spelled out, including the ones left at their default. A
setting shown as
.RB \(lq (none\ \(em\ ...) \(rq
resolved to nothing, which is how a misspelled optional key becomes visible:
you configured quiet hours and the output says there are none. PINs are shown
only as their length, never their value.
.IP
A key that matches no field in the schema is an error. This command, the
daemon's reload and
.B doorman sync
apply one validator and refuse the same files; a reload that is refused keeps
the last good policy in service, and at startup the daemon runs its last good
copy (\fIpolicy.last-good/\fR beside \fIpolicy.toml\fR) when the current files
are refused, or, with no copy yet, runs them under the previous release's
rules. Suggestions are offered \(em
.B afterhous
is told about
.BR afterhours .
Exits 0 when valid, 1 when not, naming the offending key.
.IP
On a box answering several numbers this reports every line it found \(em its
name, its file, and whether it loads \(em before describing each in turn, and
notes a PIN that appears on more than one line. It says nothing about lines at
all when there is only the default one.
.IP
With a
.I trunks.toml
it also lists the providers, which lines land on each, and \(em prominently \(em
which trunk carries a 911 call, saying whether that was
.B chosen
.RB ( emergency_trunk )
or
.B inferred
(plain
.IR policy.toml 's
.BR "[line] trunk" ),
whether that trunk declares a registered street address, and which trunks it
falls over to. It exits non\-zero when neither key has an answer: that is the
one route that may not be undecided, and
.B doorman render
refuses to generate a dialplan until it is settled. It also refuses a line
whose
.B outbound_cid
is a number this configuration declares at a different provider.
Both are silent without one, so a box with a single provider never reads the
word trunk.
.IP
With a
.I contacts.toml
it reports what the address books add up to: per source, how old the copy is
and how many vCards were read and how many numbers came out
.BR personal ,
.BR published ,
.B blocked
or
.B skipped
\(em plus which signal decided each published one, and how the conflicts
between two address books resolved. Counts only, with one exception: no name and
no number is ever printed, because "how many" answers every question this
command exists to answer. It exits non\-zero for a source whose
.B path
cannot be read, and merely reports a
.B url
source, which this release does not fetch. Silent without the file.
.IP
The exception is a number that is both allow\-listed in
.I policy.toml
and named by a block source. The block wins on the call, so the
.B [[people]]
entry does nothing \(em a contradiction for an operator to resolve rather than
a ranking to accept silently. That one exits non\-zero and prints the
allow\-list name, which the operator typed themselves, beside a redacted
number.
.IP
When the box holds the account's lobby pass book it reports it as counts: the
book's id and revision, how many holders and numbers it lists, and per line
how many of those numbers it admits, how many that line's
.B [[people]]
already names (which wins), and how many a block source shuts out. Never a
name or a number. Account books the shared rules refuse \(em a pass book a
handset selects or a book includes, a second pass book \(em exit non\-zero.
Silent when the box holds no pass book.
This is the authority on whether a configuration is usable.
.TP
.BI "doorman schema [" name ]
Print the configuration surface as JSON Schema (draft 2020\-12) on stdout:
every key, type, pattern, default, and cross\-file reference for
.IR policy.toml ,
.IR handsets.toml ,
.IR trunks.toml ,
.IR contacts.toml ,
and the environment.
.I name
is
.BR policy ,
.BR handsets ,
.BR trunks ,
.BR contacts ,
or
.BR env ;
all of them are emitted as one bundle when omitted.
.IP
This exists for tooling and for language models. It describes
.IR shape ;
it is not a validity oracle. JSON Schema cannot express cross\-file
identifier references, mutually exclusive keys, or the roughly thirty
semantic rules the validator applies \(em those appear as
.B x\-cross\-references
and
.B x\-rules
annotations, and the document says so itself. Run
.B doorman check
before relying on a config. See also
.I llms.txt
in the repository root.
.TP
.BI "doorman rotate [\-policy " path "] [\-handsets " path "] [" label ...]
Generate new extension PINs with
.IR crypto/rand ,
rewriting
.I policy.toml
in place with comments intact. Rotates every extension when no labels are
given, or only the named ones. A running daemon picks the change up within a
second. New PINs are printed to stdout \(em the one place they are ever
shown \(em and are never written to the logs.
.IP
.BI "doorman rotate \-voicemail [\-env " path "] [" box ...]
Set, or rotate, the PIN of each mailbox the handsets name \(em
.BI VOICEMAIL_ <BOX> _PIN
in
.IR .env ,
shown once. The phone that owns a box never types it;
.B *98
from another phone does. Takes effect at the next
.BR "doorman render" .
.TP
.BI "doorman rotate \-phones [\-env " path "] [" id ...]
rotates handset SIP passwords instead, rewriting
.I .env
atomically beside a timestamped backup and printing none of them: after
.B doorman render
and a reload,
.B doorman provision notify
hands each phone its new one.
.TP
.BI "doorman render [\-handsets " path "] [\-policy " path "] [\-trunks " path "] [\-contacts " path "] [\-out " dir "] [\-env " path ]
Generate the per\-handset Asterisk configuration from
.IR handsets.toml :
PJSIP endpoints, internal numbers, BLF hints, and the ring\-all and page\-all
groups. This makes the inventory authoritative over the dialplan so the two
cannot drift. Handset SIP passwords are read from the environment or
.I .env
via each handset's
.B password_env
key; the generated file contains real secrets and must be treated as such.
.IP
A handset that also carries
.B mac
and
.B model
(both or neither) is one the phone side can be generated for as well:
.B doorman render
writes that phone's own configuration file beside the PJSIP fragments and
.B doorman provision
hands it to the phone, so a new handset is two touches rather than a web
form. Its web\-admin and provisioning credentials come from
.B HANDSET_<NAME>_ADMIN_PASSWORD
and
.BR HANDSET_<NAME>_PROVISION_PASSWORD ,
generated by
.B doorman init
and never typed. The
.B phonebook
key chooses which directories the phone shows \(em
.BR house ,
.BR people ,
or a
.I contacts.toml
source id \(em and defaults to house and people.
.IP
Each endpoint also carries the outbound caller ID that phone presents, the
trunk its calls leave by and the failover ladder it climbs, read from every
line's
.BR "[line] outbound_cid" ,
.BR "[line] trunk" ,
.B [line] failover
and
.BR "outbound_handsets" .
It is generated rather than configured because a handset that picks up and
dials never reaches
.BR doorman ,
which is exactly what keeps outbound calling working when the daemon is not.
Changing either therefore takes a re\-render and a
.BR "pjsip reload" ;
the
.B *4
console picks the same change up on the next policy reload.
.IP
When a
.I trunks.toml
exists, two more files are written: the provider registrations, and a dialplan
fragment holding one inbound context per trunk with each line's DID routed to
it from
.B [line] number
and
.BR "[line] trunk" ,
matched in every digit format a provider might send, plus
.BR [cmm\-emergency] :
the trunk 911 leaves by, then every other trunk in turn so an emergency call
still connects when the designated one is down. Nothing in it presents a caller
ID. Every generated
registration carries
.B line=yes
and
.BR endpoint= ,
the pair that binds inbound calls to their endpoint \(em without both they
reach the
.B anonymous
endpoint and vanish silently. Registration passwords come from
.B password_env
exactly as handset passwords do. With no
.I trunks.toml
nothing extra is generated and a hand\-written
.I pjsip.conf
keeps working, which is every single\-provider install.
.IP
Everything written here is an
.BR output :
never hand\-edit it, and never commit the PJSIP files, which hold real
passwords.
.TP
.B doorman init services
Install the systemd units from inside the binary, create the directories
each process owns, and
.B enable \-\-now
exactly the units the configuration calls for: the daemon and the
*88/reminder housekeeping timers always; the directory with
.B PROVISION_ADDRESS
and a provisionable handset; the inbox with
.B INBOX_URL
and messages.toml; the digest with
.B MAIL_HOOK
and
.BR MAIL_TO ;
the balance timer with trunks.toml; the voicemail links with
.B VOICEMAIL_LINK_BASE
in
.IR /etc/asterisk/cmm\-mail.env .
Idempotent: a rerun is the upgrade;
a unit doorman did not write is left alone.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan. Needs root for the real thing.
.TP
.B doorman stt \fR[\fIurl\fR|\fBnone\fR]
Where the *88 spoken names are transcribed. Alone, says what is set and
whether it answers; a URL (an OpenAI\-compatible /v1/audio/transcriptions)
sets
.B STT_ENDPOINT
in .env; \fBnone\fR clears it. Only the phonebook job reads it, on its next
run, so no restart. Voicemail transcripts read their own copy from
.IR /etc/asterisk/cmm\-mail.env ,
because the asterisk user cannot read .env.
.TP
.B doorman voicemail notify \fIcontext mailbox\fR [\fInew old urgent\fR]
Asterisk's
.BR externnotify ,
run as the asterisk user and started detached by
.IR scripts/voicemail\-notify .
Mails each new message in the box's INBOX once, through
.BR MAIL_HOOK ,
to the box's own address in
.I voicemail.conf
(and the files it includes; several separated by |), else to
.BR MAIL_TO .
With
.B STT_ENDPOINT
set the mail carries a transcript of the PCM .wav, and "(no transcript)"
otherwise or on any failure. With
.B VOICEMAIL_LINK_BASE
set the recording is hard\-linked under a 128\-bit random token in
.I /var/lib/asterisk/cmm\-voicemail/links
and the mail lists one link per base instead of the file. Reads
.I /etc/asterisk/cmm\-mail.env
(or
.BR CMM_MAIL_ENV ),
never .env; exits 0 whatever happens, because mail is a feed and the
message stays in the box.
.TP
.B doorman voicemail serve
The recording links:
.B GET /v/\fItoken\fR
on
.B VOICEMAIL_LINK_LISTEN
(default :8095), as audio/wav, or a page with an audio element for a
browser. Refuses (403) any client outside loopback, RFC 1918, link\-local,
IPv6 unique\-local and the tailnet's 100.64.0.0/10; X\-Forwarded\-For is
never read. Prunes links older than
.B VOICEMAIL_LINK_DAYS
(default 14) at start and hourly. Run by
.IR doorman\-voicemail.service ;
exits 0 without
.BR VOICEMAIL_LINK_BASE .
.TP
.B doorman login \fR[\fB\-\-state\fR \fIdirectory\fR] [\fB\-\-env\fR \fIfile\fR]
Enrol this device through a browser/passkey approval. Run as the backup
service user. The device generates its own transport key and checks the
displayed code against it. Scoped credentials are saved atomically with
mode 0600 under the state directory's account subdirectory, excluded from
backups. Set \fBBACKUP_CLOUD=true\fR to opt in to encrypted uploads.
The request suggests a name for the box:
.B BOX_NAME
from the environment or the \fB\-\-env\fR file (default
/opt/call\-me\-maybe/.env), otherwise the hostname; none for
.IR localhost .
The household can rename it on the account.
.TP
.B doorman account \fBstatus\fR|\fBrotate\fR
Show identifiers, capabilities and enrolment time, never credential values.
\fBstatus \-\-json\fR is machine readable. \fBrotate \-\-capability
backup|sync\fR
persists a pending successor before first use commits it. No predecessor
fallback occurs after an ambiguous first use; invalid credentials require login.
When the service answers \fBbox_removed\fR (the owner removed this box from
its account), every credential is forgotten and \fBstatus\fR says so, with
the time. Calls are unaffected; \fBdoorman login\fR enrols the box again,
as a new box, and clears the state. \fBdoorman check\fR shows it too.
.TP
.B doorman sync \fBrun\fR|\fBonce\fR|\fBsnapshot\fR
Observe validated files into a private SQLite echo, publish secret-free
snapshots, and receive typed changes with durable receipts. Requires account
enrolment. \fBrun\fR observes every second and polls every five seconds,
backing off with jitter to five minutes (or the service's Retry\-After) while
passes fail; an unchanged snapshot is not re\-sent.
The status report carries the suggested box name
.RB ( BOX_NAME ,
otherwise the hostname) until one report carrying it has been accepted for the
enrolment, recorded in the sync state directory, and never again after that.
With the sync capability, \fBrun\fR also keeps the \fIbell\fR: an outbound,
normally quiet WebSocket on which the service only says "sync now". A ring
runs the ordinary pass at once (rings during a pass merge into one more pass);
a ping every 25 seconds keeps it alive and a missing pong reconnects it, with
backoff from one second to five minutes. While the bell is up, polling slows to
.B SYNC_BELL_POLL_S
(default 300 seconds); while it is down, refused or not offered, polling is
every five seconds as before. Nothing on the socket is configuration or a
command. A refused credential is not retried until it changes; only state
changes are logged.
.B SYNC_BELL=false
turns the bell off.
\fBonce\fR performs one exchange; \fBsnapshot\fR prints the redacted snapshot
(contact names and numbers are included, so treat this output as private).
The state directory defaults to /var/lib/doorman; \fB\-\-state\fR overrides it.
Content inventory is measured every 30 seconds from <state>/content/library;
\fB\-\-content\fR overrides the root. Reports contain relative names, import
provenance and measured sizes, never media bodies or implied phone mounts.
Policy, handset, trunk and contact inventory paths accept explicit flags.
The \fB\-\-phonebooks\fR directory defaults to PHONEBOOK_DIR or
<state>/phonebook. Handset-filed and shared imported cards enter the snapshot
as directory additions; they never become call-admission rules.
Books the account manages arrive as typed deliveries and are kept under
<phonebooks>/account/acct\-<id>.toml: directory only, composed with the
house when a phone's directory is rendered, never edited by hand (a changed
copy is reported as a conflict rather than overwritten).
One account book may carry
.BR "role = \(dqlobby\-pass\(dq" :
the lobby pass book, the reverse of the others. It is never shown in a
directory \(em no handset may select it, no book may include it, and it
includes nothing \(em and it is the one book call admission reads: a caller
whose number it lists skips the lobby, welcomed by the contact's name, after
a block source and
.B [[people]]
have had their say. A box holds at most one, of up to 5000 contacts (other
books keep 2000). It is delivered, echoed (with its role), replaced and
removed exactly as any book is, and the daemon reloads it within a second,
keeping the last good one when the books are refused (with a copy at
<phonebooks>/account/.lobby\-pass.last\-good for a restart). Its receipt is
applied once the daemon admits from exactly the delivered bytes.
A queue item may
carry an ordered list of verbs, applied together with one receipt; a refused
verb writes nothing.
Policy schedule edits and phonebook assignments validate the real files,
preserve unrelated TOML and comments, and use ordinary automatic hot reload.
Invalid or conflicting input leaves the working files unchanged.
Saved receipts become applied only when the running daemon confirms the
compiled file revision through its private Unix socket. This is not proof
that a handset has refreshed its screen. The sync ledger is excluded from
backups; a replacement enrols independently.
A removed box logs one line and exits 6, which doorman\-sync.service treats
as a clean stop: no restart, no retry against the service. After
\fBdoorman login\fR, start the unit again.
.TP
.B doorman sync \fBinventory\fR
Print a read-only local library report without account enrolment or network
access. Accepts \fB\-\-state\fR and \fB\-\-content\fR.
Missing roots and unreadable
entries remain explicitly unavailable or partial, not zero-sized libraries.
See docs/CONTENT-INVENTORY.md for the optional
local .content-source.json import record.
.TP
.B doorman backup \fBinit\fR|\fBrun\fR|\fBlist\fR|\fBverify\fR
The house as one sealed file. \fBinit\fR makes the keypair: the public half
into .env as
.BR BACKUP_RECIPIENT ,
the private half printed once and never stored. \fBrun\fR bundles .env, the
TOMLs, the state directory (the journal snapshotted), voicemail, the *88
spool and the hand-written Asterisk files, age\-encrypted, and delivers it
to every configured file/S3 destination, pruning by
.BR BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY / WEEKLY ;
doorman\-backup.timer runs it nightly. \fBverify\fR opens the newest with
the private key (\fBBACKUP_IDENTITY_FILE\fR, or typed).
\fBBACKUP_CLOUD=true\fR additionally uploads through the enrolled account.
Cloud retention belongs to the service; upload credentials cannot list,
read or delete bundles. Successful cloud delivery reports stored, not verified.
On a box removed from its account the cloud destination reports stopped,
not failed, and asks the service nothing; file and S3 copies continue. If
cloud was the only destination nothing was backed up, and the run exits 1.
View metadata at https://app.dialdoorman.cc/backups/.
Printed-phrase recovery uses the separate recovery commands below.
.TP
.B doorman recovery authorize|setup|restore|confirm|cancel|status
Printed-phrase cloud recovery. Enrol the device independently, then authorize
with a separate account passkey approval. Setup prints a private HTML sheet
path;
save or print it and confirm by entering its new phrase at the hidden prompt.
Restore takes the old phrase and requires a new absolute \fB\-\-root\fR;
it prepares a replacement sheet before confirmation stages and verifies files.
Use root for confirmation to restore recorded owners. No services are started
and no source device is retired. \fB\-\-phrase-file\fR accepts a private
regular
file instead of terminal input; never put a phrase in command arguments.
Cancel abandons only an unconfirmed operation and keeps the current phrase.
See docs/CLOUD-BACKUP-PROTOCOL.md for interruption recovery and limits.
.TP
.B doorman restore \fIbundle\fR|\fB\-\-latest\fR
A box from a bundle. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR shows the manifest and what would
be written; a box with a .env is refused unless \fB\-\-force\fR (it is
backed up first); \fB\-\-root\fR \fIdir\fR rehearses elsewhere. Then check,
render, init services, start doorman.
.TP
.B doorman lsp
Run a language server over stdio for
.IR policy.toml ,
.I handsets.toml
and
.IR trunks.toml .
Diagnostics come from the same validator that guards the daemon, so unknown
handset ids, bad schedule references, and duplicate PINs are underlined as
you type, with completions for every cross\-reference. Works in Neovim over
SSH on the Pi with no additional installation. See
.IR docs/editor.md .
.TP
.B doorman calls
.RS
Read the call log: one line per completed call, with who called, what happened
and why. Numbers are redacted unless
.BR \-\-no\-redact ,
so the default output is safe to paste into a bug report or hand to a model.
Entered digits and PINs are never recorded at all \(em a record says whether a
PIN was valid, never what it was. Requires
.B CALL_LOG_PATH
to be set; see
.BR "doorman schema env" .
.PP
A box answering several numbers writes one log with
.B line
on each record, not one file per line, so a whole day still reads in order. A
call placed through the
.B *4
console is recorded too, as
.BR direction " " outbound
with the number in
.BR dialled ;
its outcome is
.B placed
rather than
.BR answered ,
because doorman hands the channel to the dialplan and never learns whether
anybody picked up. Both fields are absent for the default line and for inbound,
so a box with one number reads exactly as it always did \(em no
.B LINE
column, no direction column.
.PP
Filters:
.BR \-\-since " (a duration like " 24h " or " 7d ", or a date), "
.BR \-\-outcome ", " \-\-caller " (either end of the call), "
.BR \-\-line " (" default " is plain policy.toml), "
.BR \-\-direction ", " \-n .
.B \-\-json
emits JSON Lines.
.RE
.TP
.BI "doorman provision [\-window " d "] [\-forever] [\-all] [\-state " dir "] [" id ...]
A phone configures itself. With no ids, the inventory view: every handset
with its model, MAC, the file it fetches, and whether it is registered right
now (read over ARI on loopback) \(em no listener. With ids or
.BR \-all :
render, open a bounded HTTPS window on
.B PROVISION_ADDRESS
with a certificate minted once into the state directory, answer only the
listed MACs \(em the first fetch open, every later one with that phone's own
credential \(em print exactly what to type on each phone, and watch
connect, fetch and registration as they happen. A fetch from a MAC that is not
in the inventory is refused, and the refusal prints the MAC to paste into
.IR handsets.toml .
Exit 0 when every named phone registered, 1 when the window closed first, 2
when the inventory or the flags were wrong. Ctrl\-C closes the window
cleanly.
.B \-\-models
lists the model ids;
.B \-\-export\-cert
prints the certificate for a phone that validates servers.
.IP
.BI "doorman provision notify [" id ...]
opens the window and sends a check\-sync NOTIFY through the Asterisk console
so registered phones fetch again \(em the second half of a rotation.
.IP
.B doorman provision directory
is the phones' directory: the house, the allow\-list and any
.I contacts.toml
source a handset opted into, served always, read\-only, one port above the
window, with each phone's own credential, re\-read when the inputs change.
It never serves a configuration.
.B doorman\-directory.service
runs it.
.TP
.BI "doorman inbox [\-messages " path "] [\-env " path "] [\-state " dir "] [\-once] [\-wait " d ]
Texts to the house number. Long\-polls the house's edge inbox \(em the
Cloudflare Worker in
.I edge/
that the carrier calls back, named by
.B INBOX_URL
and
.B INBOX_TOKEN
\(em acts on the words
.I messages.toml
allows from the
.B [[people]]
ids it names, and replies through the edge, which holds the carrier API key
so this box never does. One number, one purpose: control plane and archive,
never conversation; strangers and unknown words are never answered. Prints
each outcome with the sender redacted and never the text. Exits 0 with
nothing to do when
.B INBOX_URL
or
.I messages.toml
is absent;
.B doorman\-inbox.service
runs it for good. With
.BR MAIL_HOOK " and " MAIL_TO
set, every reply the house sends is copied to the house mailbox, and its own
outcome log (in the state directory) records what each text did. With
.BR HA_URL " and " HA_TOKEN
set, the question form of a word (\fIgarage?\fR) reads the entity the
action's
.B state
names from Home Assistant and answers with it, and an action whose
.B done_when
state already holds is left alone ("It was already open"); an action that
names a state with neither key set is refused at startup. With
.B EVENT_JOURNAL_PATH
set, every text and action is written to the journal beside the daemon's
events (message.received, message.acted, action.performed, action.refused)
as a second writer that holds no owner lock.
.PP
A messages.toml word may set
.B phonebook = "house"
or
.B phonebook = "handset:<id>"
to import attached vCards into the house directory or one handset's own
book. Follow the cards promptly with instructions such as
\fBAdd to: house,kitchen\fR to name one or more configured words,
ignoring case and spaces around commas.
An attachment caption also works; a bare word still selects one book.
Uncaptioned cards wait 15 seconds, then a notice offers two minutes to replace
the permitted House default. Timely instructions select only the named books;
cancel discards the batch. Late instructions ask for a fresh card and never
apply to the next one. Pending cards and explicit choices survive restarts.
Every destination must exist and permit
the sender before anything is saved. The Worker downloads and parses the
attachments; the house pulls only bounded name/number pairs, validates them
again, and never receives the original file or its URL. Only those words'
permitted people may import. Names and numbers are saved, duplicates skipped,
and the sender receives a count. No call admission changes. At most three
attachments, 1300 KiB each, and 100 distinct numbers. This cannot be combined
with action, webhook or reply. Requires edge MMS forwarding and a live carrier
contact-card delivery test; see RUNBOOK.
.TP
.BI "doorman digest [\-since " d "] [\-full] [\-mail] [\-subject " s "] [\-env " path "] [\-inbox\-state " dir ]
Yesterday as one mail: the day's calls from the journal (or the legacy log)
and its texts from the inbox's outcome log, rendered as Markdown. Printed to
stdout redacted, as
.B doorman calls
is;
.B \-\-full
shows whole numbers;
.B \-\-mail
hands it to
.B MAIL_HOOK
for
.BR MAIL_TO .
A query over the journal, never an input to anything, never on a call.
.B doorman\-digest.timer
runs it every morning with
.BR "\-\-mail \-\-full" ,
because the house mailbox is the audit log and already holds whole numbers.
.TP
.B doorman balance
What is left on each prepaid trunk, per trunk, with a non\-zero exit when any
of it is below its threshold \(em so a cron entry needs no wrapper script. A
prepaid account that reaches zero does not error: inbound calls simply stop
arriving, and "nobody called today" is indistinguishable from a quiet Tuesday.
.PP
Balance is a capability rather than a provider feature. A prepaid provider
implements it; an invoiced one does not, and is reported as
.RB \(lq "postpaid \(em no balance to report" \(rq
rather than as a zero that looks alarming or a blank that looks broken. A
provider doorman has no client for is reported too, never silently skipped.
.PP
Needs a
.IR trunks.toml ;
without one it says there is nothing to check and exits 0. The credentials come
from
.B api_username
and
.B api_password_env
on each trunk \(em or
.B api_key_env
instead, for a provider whose API takes one key, such as Telnyx \(em and the
threshold from
.B balance_min
there or
.B \-\-min
here.
.B doorman check
refuses a trunk whose credentials are the shape its provider does not take.
.PP
.BR "The daemon never reads those credentials" .
A provider API key manages DIDs, sub\-accounts and billing, which is
considerably more privilege than the SIP sub\-account password; keeping the
check in a CLI keeps that key off the call path and out of the long\-running
process. Run it wherever alerting already lives rather than on the Pi \(em
.B doorman balance \-\-json
needs a copy of
.I trunks.toml
and the API password, and no daemon at all.
.PP
.BI "\-\-ring " ids
rings a handset when a trunk is low \(em a handset or group id from
.IR handsets.toml ,
several in order, the first to answer hears it \(em and the daemon says
"the calling credit is running low, it is down to about" and the lowest low
balance as a whole number. An internal call never touches a trunk, so a dead
account can still ring the kitchen to say it is dead. It needs the daemon on
this box (the call is placed over ARI, on loopback, with
.BR ARI_USERNAME " and " ARI_PASSWORD
from
.IR .env ),
and it rings once per trunk per
.B \-\-repeat
(default 24h), remembered in
.B \-\-state
(default
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/doorman/balance.json ).
.B BALANCE_RING
in the environment is the same as the flag.
.PP
.BI "\-\-prom " file
writes the Prometheus text format for node_exporter's textfile collector on
every run, atomically: balance, threshold and whether the last check read a
number, per trunk, labelled by trunk id and provider and never by an
account. The daemon never serves a balance because it never checks one.
.B BALANCE_PROM
in the environment is the same as the flag.
.B doorman\-balance.timer
runs the command every morning.
.PP
Flags:
.BR \-\-trunks ", " \-\-env ", " \-\-min ", " \-\-json ", " \-\-timeout ", "
.BR \-\-ring ", " \-\-handsets ", " \-\-repeat ", " \-\-ring\-timeout ", " \-\-state ", " \-\-prom .
.TP
.BI "doorman numbers [\-trunks " path "] [\-json] [" policy ]
Which phone numbers each trunk's account holds, asked of the provider's API,
against the numbers the policy files claim for that trunk:
.B [line] number
and
.B [line] outbound_cid
on every line whose
.B [line] trunk
names it. Each claim is reported as on the account and assigned to the
connection the trunk registers as; on the account but assigned to no
connection, or to one another SIP username registers as; not active yet; or
not on the account at all. Numbers on the account that no policy file names
are listed too, and one claimed for another trunk on the same account names
that trunk. A number a line only presents needs to be held, not routed.
.PP
Telnyx today, through
.B api_key_env
on the trunk. A provider doorman cannot ask (VoIP.ms) is reported with its
claims unchecked, never failed. The numbers are the household's own and are
printed in full, as
.B doorman check
prints them. The key can buy and release numbers;
.BR "the daemon never reads it" .
.PP
Flags:
.BR \-\-trunks ", " \-\-handsets ", " \-\-env ", " \-\-json ", " \-\-timeout .
Exit status as for
.BR "doorman balance" :
1 when a claimed number is not where the policy says, 3 when a trunk could not
be checked \(em including one whose API credentials are not the kind its
provider takes. A missing policy file is exit 2: with no claims, every number
would otherwise read as unclaimed and the run would pass.
.PP
.BI "doorman e164 " number
Show how a raw caller ID normalises to E.164. Use this when a known caller
unexpectedly meets the bouncer: the carrier may be handing over a format
that does not match what is written in the allow\-list.
.TP
.B doorman version
Print the version. A release binary reports its git tag.
.TP
.B doorman help
Print a usage summary.
.TP
.B doorman pack check <dir> [--hashes]
Validate source without rendering. With
.B --hashes
print the source hashes used to bind recorded clips to their scripts.
.TP
.B doorman pack build <dir> [flags]
Render source to audio using the manifest's backend.
.B -o
selects the output directory (default <dir>/build).
.B --archive <file.cmmpack>
also packages a distributable ZIP with source, audio and checksums.
.B --source-only
requires --archive and packages scripts plus declared recordings without
synthesis.
.B --dry-run
reports the work without writing or rendering.
.B --backend <name>
overrides the backend only for loose directory builds, not archive output.
.TP
.B doorman pack voices --backend <name>
List voices offered by a configured backend: piper, exec, elevenlabs, openai
or polly.
.TP
.B doorman pack lint <file.cmmpack>
Validate a version-1 ZIP, checksums, source and supplied audio without
rendering.
Only voice and story kinds are supported. Unknown versions, unsafe paths/links,
undeclared payload, corrupt audio and incomplete bundled pairs fail validation.
Audio is mono 16-bit PCM: 8 kHz .wav and 16 kHz .wav16.
Level warnings use RMS and sample peak; see docs/PACKS.md for tolerances.
.TP
.B doorman pack install <file.cmmpack> [flags]
Validate, stage, render missing clips, verify and atomically install an
immutable
version. No account or hosted service is required. Bundled and valid cached
audio
skip synthesis. Progress and completed renders survive retries; installation
does
not change the active voice. Rendering never occurs on the call path.
.B --dry-run
reports bundled/cached/missing counts and character/request estimates only.
.B --sounds-dir <dir>
selects the Asterisk sounds root (default /var/lib/asterisk/sounds).
.B --env <file>
selects operator configuration (default .env; process environment takes
precedence).
.B --renderer-revision <id>
controls cache invalidation (default this doorman build's version); change
it after
replacing local models or renderer configuration not identified by the manifest.
Credentials and executable configuration are never taken from downloaded packs.
.TP
.B doorman pack list [--json] [--sounds-dir <dir>] [--env <file>]
Show installed versions, latest staged/rendering/failed/installed attempts and
the configured default prefix. An interrupted job can remain staged/rendering
until retried. An installed upgrade is inactive until explicitly selected.
JSON exposes local progress and provenance for future delivery adapters.
.TP
.B doorman pack use <id> [--sounds-dir <dir>] [--env <file>] [--no-restart]
Verify a complete installed voice, atomically update PROMPT_MEDIA_PREFIX and
restart a running doorman.service. The service must read the specified
environment
file. If stopped, it takes the selection on its next start. Restart failure
restores
the old configuration and attempts to restart it; recovery failures are
reported.
.B --no-restart
saves configuration only; restart your supervisor yourself to activate it.
Per-line prompts overrides still take precedence. The bundled voice and its
system phrases are never overwritten. Story packs install alongside voices;
their handset player is separate work and they cannot be selected as lobby
voices.
.PP
Pack playback versions live under sounds/doorman-packs/<id>/versions/;
private cache, provenance and progress live under sounds/.doorman-packs/.
Old versions are retained. Never remove a version referenced by the default
or a per-line prefix. doorman check reports the configured default, not a
live playback probe. See docs/PACKS.md and docs/STORY-PACKS.md for the archive,
source/audio bindings, cache rules and audio level targets.
.SH SANDBOX PREVIEW MEDIA
.B doorman sync media list|once --source https://trusted-sandbox-origin
runs the explicit, command-side preview lane. The approved origin receives the
box's sync credential; redirects and cross-origin download addresses
are refused.
It is separate from configuration sync and never runs on call admission.
.B list
shows offers without signed URLs.
.B once
verifies and installs bounded previews into the local Books library,
with durable
installation receipts. Options are --state and --library. No timer is installed.
.PP
.B doorman books install-preview --archive local.cmmpack
uses the same free local importer without an account. Previews are limited to
1 MiB archives and two minutes. Full books require compressed playback support;
this importer deliberately refuses them. An installed receipt is not proof of
handset playback. See docs/media-preview.md for limits and recovery semantics.
.PP
.B doorman sync media list|once --source https://trusted-sandbox-origin --audiobook-sku sandbox-recorded-book-v1
explicitly negotiates a selected complete audiobook through the v2 media format.
Downloads stream to private disk and are size/hash verified before installation.
The importer retains compressed Opus segments and bounds decoding, archive
expansion and playback windows; it never expands the complete book to PCM.
Supply --menu-bundle when the next catalog needs matching
offline-prepared prompts.
.PP
.B doorman books install-audiobook --archive recorded.cmmpack
uses that free full-book importer without an account or commerce receipts.
Archives are capped at 256 MiB, expanded members at 512 MiB, and recordings at
48 hours. The separate preview importer keeps its existing limits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I policy.toml
The rules: line identity, allow\-list, extensions, ringer ladders, schedules.
An extension's
.B afterhours
names one
.B [[schedules]]
id or a list of them, and the line is quiet while any is active \(em
weekdays 9\(en5 is
.BR "afterhours = [\(dqnights\(dq, \(dqweekend\-days\(dq]" .
Changes weekly and is safe to hand\-edit \(em when
.B POLICY_WATCH
is enabled, edits go live without a restart, and a file that fails
validation is logged and discarded while the last good policy stays in
service. Contains PINs; keep it mode 0600.
.TP
.I handsets.toml
The hardware inventory: what phones exist, their ids, endpoints, internal
numbers, and which
.I .env
variable holds each SIP password. Changes when hardware is bought.
.TP
.I trunks.toml
.BR Optional .
The providers: hosts, sub\-accounts, codecs, and which trunk carries 911.
Changes when you buy a number somewhere new. Not having it is the normal
state \(em one provider fits in a hand\-written
.I pjsip.conf
and nothing is generated for it. Holds no secrets:
.B password_env
names a
.I .env
variable, and the loader refuses a value that is not shaped like one.
.TP
.I contacts.toml
.BR Optional .
The address books: which vCard exports to read, and which of them is a block
list. Not having it is the normal state, and with no file nothing is read and
nothing is printed. Holds no secrets \(em
.B token_env
names a
.I .env
variable \(em but the exports it points at hold entire address books and
belong outside any repository, mode 0600. With one, the lobby walks a ladder
and the first match wins: a block source (dismissed, and never hears the
lobby), then
.BR [[people]] ,
then the account's lobby pass book when the box holds one, then a personal
contact (all straight through), then a published contact and everybody else
(the lobby). Nothing here is authoritative:
.B [[people]]
in
.I policy.toml
stays the deliberate list and beats the classifier, and deleting every source
changes nothing but who hears the lobby.
.TP
.I .env
Secrets and tuning. Loaded by systemd's
.B EnvironmentFile
in production. Contains the ARI password and every handset and trunk
password; keep it mode 0600.
.TP
.I /etc/asterisk/cmm\-mail.env
The voicemail mail's settings, read by
.B doorman voicemail
as the asterisk user, which cannot read .env: MAIL_TO, MAIL_HOOK, a copy of
STT_ENDPOINT, STT_MODEL and STT_TIMEOUT_MS, and VOICEMAIL_LINK_BASE,
VOICEMAIL_LINK_LISTEN and VOICEMAIL_LINK_DAYS. Root\-owned, group asterisk,
mode 0640.
.TP
.I examples/
Templates to copy for all of the above.
.TP
.I /var/lib/asterisk/sounds/<prefix>/
Pre\-rendered prompt audio, where
.I <prefix>
is
.BR PROMPT_MEDIA_PREFIX .
Prompts are built once with piper on a workstation and committed as WAVs, so
the Pi never synthesises speech. Pointing the prefix at another directory is
how a prompt pack is swapped in. A line's own words sit beneath its prefix
in
.I menus/<id>
and
.IR recordings/<id> ,
operator content outside any pack; run on the box,
.B doorman check
warns about the ones that are missing.
.TP
.I /var/lib/asterisk/sounds/call\-me\-maybe/challenge/
The arithmetic challenge's clips, rendered with the bundled pack by
.I prompts/build.sh
and played whatever pack a line speaks with.
.TP
.I /var/lib/asterisk/sounds/call\-me\-maybe/system/listen\-*
Dial\-a\-Story's warning, goodbye and busy phrases, bundled system clips
rendered the same way.
.SH UPDATE NOTICES
Successful interactive operator commands may print a newer-release notice on
stderr after command output, at most once per 24 hours. Both stdout and stderr
must be terminals. Checks overlap the command within a one-second startup
budget. Failures are silent; failed commands never print notices. The daemon,
LSP, CI, pipes and source builds (git distance or dirty stamps) never check.
Only strictly newer semantic versions are advertised. Nothing auto-updates.
Upgrade using install.sh on a workstation; on the Pi, download and verify
the release binary per docs/RUNBOOK.md.
State is stored in $XDG_STATE_HOME/doorman/update-check.json, defaulting to
~/.local/state/doorman/update-check.json (0600 file, 0700 directory).
A successful fetch is cached even before the command finishes, for later runs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B UPDATE_CHECK_ENABLED
Boolean, default true. Set false to disable the interactive release check
without loading or validating daemon configuration. Truthy values are
1, true, yes and on (case-insensitive); other non-empty values disable it.
A non-empty CI also disables checks.
.B doorman schema env
prints all of these with types, defaults, and explanations. The frequently
relevant ones:
.TP
.BR ARI_USERNAME ", " ARI_PASSWORD
Required. Must match
.IR asterisk/ari.conf .
.TP
.B ARI_BASE_URL
Default
.IR http://127.0.0.1:8088 .
Keep ARI bound to loopback.
.TP
.B ARI_APP
Default
.IR doorman .
Must match the
.B Stasis()
application named in
.IR asterisk/extensions.conf .
.TP
.BR POLICY_PATH ", " HANDSETS_PATH ", " TRUNKS_PATH ", " CONTACTS_PATH ", " POLICY_WATCH
Where the config files are, and whether to reload them on change.
.TP
.B BOX_NAME
The name this box suggests to its connected account; unset suggests the
hostname, and
.I localhost
or an empty name suggests none. Trimmed and cut on a character boundary to at
most 64 characters. Sent by
.B doorman login
at enrolment and once in a sync status report; after it is accepted the
account owns the name, so changing this renames nothing. Only ever sent to the
household's own connected account; never logged.
.TP
.BR SYNC_BELL ", " SYNC_BELL_POLL_S
Read by
.B doorman sync run
only. The sync bell is on by default for a box enrolled with the sync
capability; false turns it off and sync polls every five seconds exactly as
before. While the bell is up, sync polls every
.B SYNC_BELL_POLL_S
seconds (default 300, 5 to 3600) as a safety net.
.I CONTACTS_PATH
is optional and a missing file is the normal state, in which nothing is read,
nothing is logged, and the lobby admits exactly who
.B [[people]]
says it admits. With a file, the daemon compiles the merged set at startup and
the lobby consults it per call; an inventory that will not load warns and the
phone keeps answering.
.I TRUNKS_PATH
is optional and a missing file is the normal state; the daemon reads it only
to announce which trunk carries 911 and to warn about a caller ID its own
trunk does not own. Nothing on the call path consults it, so a
.I trunks.toml
that will not load warns and the phone keeps answering.
.B POLICY_PATH
also anchors the search for extra lines: any
.I policy.<line>.toml
beside it configures the line of that name, which the dialplan selects with
.BR "Stasis(app,line,<name>)" .
Each line gets its own store, so one bad file cannot take down another line.
Bare
.I policy.toml
is the default line and serves every call that names none.
A new line file is picked up at startup; edits to an existing one are live.
.TP
.B PROMPT_MEDIA_PREFIX
Default
.IR call\-me\-maybe .
The prompt pack every line speaks from unless it names its own with
.BR "[line] prompts" ,
which overrides this per line and is read from the policy file, so swapping a
pack reloads like any other policy edit. The house's own phrases \(em the
low\-balance call \(em always come from the bundled pack's
.I system/
directory, whatever this says: a pack supplies the six lobby prompts and
cannot silence an alert.
.TP
.BI VOICEMAIL_ <BOX> _PIN
The PIN of each mailbox a handset names, read by
.B doorman render
into
.I voicemail_handsets.conf
exactly as
.BI HANDSET_ <ID> _PASSWORD
is read into the PJSIP file.
.B doorman init
writes one per room;
.B doorman rotate \-\-voicemail
sets or rotates them. A box with no variable is left to the hand\-written
.IR voicemail.conf .
.TP
.BR MAIL_HOOK ", " MAIL_TO
Read by
.BR "doorman inbox" " and " "doorman digest"
and never by the daemon: the executable that sends one mail \(em subject as
its argument, Markdown body on stdin,
.B MAIL_TO
in its environment, non\-zero exit when it did not send \(em and the house
mailbox address. The shipped
.I scripts/mail\-hook\-bullmoose
runs the bullmoose CLI with a token scoped to draft and send. Both or
neither. Asterisk's own voicemail hook reads the same two names from
.IR /etc/asterisk/cmm\-mail.env .
.TP
.BR BALANCE_RING ", " BALANCE_PROM
Read by
.B doorman balance
and never by the daemon: the handset or group ids to ring when a trunk is
low, and the Prometheus textfile to write. Empty means neither. See
.BR "doorman balance" .
.TP
.B FIRST_DIGIT_TIMEOUT_MS
Default 10000. Time to dial the
.I first
digit, measured from the end of the greeting; a stranger reading a PIN off a
card needs longer than a stopwatch allows.
.B INTER_DIGIT_TIMEOUT_MS
(default 3000) governs the gap between subsequent digits.
.TP
.BR RATELIMIT_ENABLED ", " RATELIMIT_MAX_FAILURES ", " RATELIMIT_WINDOW_MS
After repeated failures, a caller skips the greeting entirely and goes
straight to dismissal, leaving nothing to brute\-force against.
.TP
.BR LOG_LEVEL ", " LOG_FORMAT ", " LOG_REDACT_CALLER_ID
.B trace|debug|info|warn|error
,
.B json|pretty
, and whether to log only the last four digits of unknown callers.
.TP
.BR WEBHOOK_URL ", " WEBHOOK_REDACT_CALLER_ID
Unset by default. With a URL,
.B doorman
POSTs one JSON event when the house starts ringing and one when a call ends,
so Home Assistant can announce the caller or flash a light. The payload names
no speaker and no routing \(em Home Assistant decides who hears it. Delivery
is off the call path and can never delay a call. Caller IDs are redacted
unless the second variable is set false; entered digits and PINs are never
sent at any setting. Treat the URL as a credential: a Home Assistant webhook
id is the whole secret, and
.B doorman
logs only its host.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed \(em an invalid configuration, an unreachable ARI, an
unwritable output directory.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.PP
.B doorman balance
and
.B doorman numbers
use the same 0, 1 and 2 and add one:
.TP
.B 3
Nothing was below its threshold (or, for
.BR numbers ,
out of place), but at least one trunk could not be checked at all.
Separate from 1 on purpose \(em an expired API key and an empty account
look identical from here, and treating one as the other is how a real outage
gets dismissed as a broken cron job. 1 wins when both happen, because a balance
known to be low is more actionable than one that is unknown.
.PP
.B doorman sync
adds one:
.TP
.B 6
The account owner removed this box. Sync has stopped for good, on purpose;
\fBdoorman login\fR enrols the box again.
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate a configuration before trusting it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman check \-\-handsets examples/handsets.example.toml \\
              examples/policy.example.toml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a language model the whole configuration surface:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman schema > /tmp/doorman\-schema.json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Work out why a known caller met the bouncer:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman e164 "(512) 555\-0100"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rotate one extension's PIN after it has been shared too widely:
.PP
.RS
.EX
doorman rotate Kids
.EE
.RE
.PP
Watch a prepaid trunk from cron, on the machine where your alerting lives
rather than on the Pi \(em the exit code is the whole interface:
.PP
.RS
.EX
0 9 * * *  doorman balance \-\-trunks /etc/doorman/trunks.toml \\
             || notify\-me "call me maybe: trunk balance"
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Extensions are credentials. A 6\-digit PIN reachable from the PSTN is a
million combinations \(em ample against a person, thin against a machine that
can redial \(em which is why the rate limiter exists and why
.B doorman rotate
generates PINs rather than letting you choose them.
.PP
Caller IDs and PINs never appear in the logs. That invariant is enforced
mechanically by a static analysis pass in the repository
.RI ( tools/nologsecrets ),
not by convention: logging a digit
.I count
is allowed, logging the digits is not.
.PP
Withheld caller ID always meets the bouncer, and anonymous callers share a
single rate\-limit bucket. One persistent blocked caller therefore makes the
lobby stricter for every other withheld number. That is the intended trade.
.SH PUBLIC PODCAST FEEDS
.B doorman podcasts subscribe --handset id --feed-url https://publisher.example.org/show.xml --title "Show"
adds a public HTTPS source and handset subscription atomically.
.B doorman podcasts unsubscribe --handset id --feed-url URL
removes only that handset/feed pair. Use --config and --handsets for
nondefault paths.
Schema 2 permits eight generic feeds and 100 handset/feed pairs;
schema 1 preserves
the pinned Brains On pilot. Generic sync requires
podcast.subscribeFeed capability.
The background worker selects two eligible items per show; preparation failure
keeps the previous catalog. Per-feed menus and bookmarks are handset scoped;
active playback delays cache collection. See docs/PODCASTS.md for
bounds and rollout.

.PP
The podcast timer uses refresh --scheduled: existing feeds poll hourly with HTTP
validators, failed feeds retry after five minutes, and explicit refresh bypasses
the interval. Episodes are staged one at a time and failures isolated by feed.
Removing the last subscription reclaims cached audio after active menus end.
Public metadata is readable by the AGI inside protected service-owned
directories;
invalid optional podcast configuration does not stop other sync.

